# ⛔ PIN LINE ENDINGS, BECAUSE THE HARNESS HASHES ITS OWN SOURCE AND GIT REWRITES IT ON CHECKOUT.
#
# Legacy records hash `search.mjs` + `states.mjs` as checked-out bytes. V2 normalizes CRLF while
# hashing the complete instrument, but still relies on LF shell sources and byte-stable generated
# records. Without this file, the legacy hash remains a property of the checkout rather than the
# harness commit.
#
# MEASURED: identical committed source hashed 0de2d34e463a748a on the Linux and macOS runners and
# 6fa2b6be501b4b5d on the Windows runner. Re-hashing the committed bytes with \n -> \r\n reproduces
# the Windows value exactly, which is the proof rather than the theory.
#
# Two things that breaks, both silent:
#   - `--stale-harness` purges records "measured under an older harness". Run on Windows it would
#     discard every Linux and macOS record, and vice versa — the whole corpus, every time.
#   - Any claim of the form "these records share a harness revision" is false across platforms. The
#     determination of which records have a trustworthy jail-off control was keyed on the LF value
#     and would have wrongly excluded every Windows record.
#
# `eol=lf` makes the working-tree bytes identical everywhere, so the hash means what it says.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts additionally MUST be LF: bash on Windows fails a CRLF script with the famously
# unhelpful `$'\r': command not found`, pointing at whichever line happens to be first.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Records are generated JSON; keep them byte-stable so a re-commit is a no-op rather than a diff.
records/** -text
records-v2/** -text
queue.ndjson -text
queue-v2.ndjson -text
